 "drivingQuestion": "What does kevulot mean?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "kevulot glossed and grounded in I Kings 9:13.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.54a.2",
   "daf": "54a",
   "sentenceId": 1,
   "he": "הָרְחֵלִים יוֹצְאוֹת כְּבוּלוֹת. מַאי ״כְּבוּלוֹת״? — שֶׁמְּכַבְּלִין אַלְיָה שֶׁלָּהֶן לְמַטָּה כְּדֵי שֶׁלֹּא יַעֲלוּ עֲלֵיהֶן זְכָרִים. מַאי מַשְׁמַע דְּהַאי ״כָּבוּל״ לִישָּׁנָא דְּלָא עָבֵיד פֵּירֵי הוּא — דִּכְתִיב: ״מָה הֶעָרִים [הָאֵלֶּה] אֲשֶׁר נָתַתָּ לִּי אָחִי וַיִּקְרָא לָהֶן אֶרֶץ כָּבוּל עַד הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה״.",
   "en": "We learned in the mishna: «Ewes may go out kevulot.» The Gemara asks: «What is» the meaning of «kevulot?» It means «that they bind their tails down» with animal hide «so that the males will not mount them.» The Gemara explains: «From where» may it «be inferred that this» word «kavul is a term» meaning «does not produce fruit? As it is written,» when Solomon gave a portion of land to Hiram, he complained: «“What cities are these which you have given me, my brother? And he called them the land of Kavul to this day”» (I Kings 9:13)."
  }
 ],
